<quiz>
<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Interquartile range and quartile deviation by calculation</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>The table shows the milk sold, in litres (to the nearest litre), on \({@n@}\) days at a dairy kiosk in Nakuru.</p><table border="1" style="border-collapse:collapse;text-align:center;"><tr><th style="padding:4px 10px;">Milk (litres)</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">{@lo[1]@}–{@hi[1]@}</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">{@lo[2]@}–{@hi[2]@}</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">{@lo[3]@}–{@hi[3]@}</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">{@lo[4]@}–{@hi[4]@}</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">{@lo[5]@}–{@hi[5]@}</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">{@lo[6]@}–{@hi[6]@}</th></tr><tr><td style="padding:4px 10px;">Frequency (\(f\))</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@f[1]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@f[2]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@f[3]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@f[4]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@f[5]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@f[6]@}</td></tr></table><p>For grouped data, a quartile, decile or percentile at a given position is estimated by \[ L + \frac{\text{position} - CF}{F} \times c \]where \(L\) is the lower class boundary of the class that holds the position, \(CF\) the cumulative frequency before that class, \(F\) its frequency and \(c\) the class width.</p><p>Give each answer correct to 1 decimal place.</p><p>(a) Find the lower quartile, \(Q_1\).</p><div>\(Q_1 =\) [[input:ans1]] litres [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div><p>(b) Find the upper quartile, \(Q_3\).</p><div>\(Q_3 =\) [[input:ans2]] litres [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div><p>(c) Find the interquartile range.</p><div>IQR \(=\) [[input:ans3]] litres [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</div><p>(d) Find the quartile deviation.</p><div>QD \(=\) [[input:ans4]] litres [[validation:ans4]] [[feedback:prt4]]</div>]]></text>
  </questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>For grouped data, \(Q_1\) is at position \(n/4\) and \(Q_3\) at \(3n/4\). The amounts are to the nearest litre, so each class starts at its lower class boundary (the class {@lo[1]@}–{@hi[1]@} starts at \({@exd(lcb[1])@}\)).</p><table border="1" style="border-collapse:collapse;text-align:center;"><tr><th style="padding:4px 10px;">Class</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">Frequency (\(f\))</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">Cumulative frequency (cf)</th></tr><tr><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@lo[1]@}–{@hi[1]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@f[1]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@cf[1]@}</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@lo[2]@}–{@hi[2]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@f[2]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@cf[2]@}</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@lo[3]@}–{@hi[3]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@f[3]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@cf[3]@}</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@lo[4]@}–{@hi[4]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@f[4]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@cf[4]@}</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@lo[5]@}–{@hi[5]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@f[5]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@cf[5]@}</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@lo[6]@}–{@hi[6]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@f[6]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@cf[6]@}</td></tr></table><p>(a) Position \({@n@} \div 4 = {@exd(p1)@}\), in the class {@lo[k1]@}–{@hi[k1]@}: \[Q_1 = {@exd(lcb[k1])@} + \frac{ {@exd(p1)@} - {@cfb(k1)@} }{ {@f[k1]@} } \times 10 \approx {@q1d@}\]</p><p>(b) Position \(3 \times {@n@} \div 4 = {@exd(p3)@}\), in the class {@lo[k3]@}–{@hi[k3]@}: \[Q_3 = {@exd(lcb[k3])@} + \frac{ {@exd(p3)@} - {@cfb(k3)@} }{ {@f[k3]@} } \times 10 \approx {@q3d@}\]</p><p>(c) The IQR is the spread of the middle half of the data. Using the unrounded quartiles, \(Q_3 - Q_1 \approx {@iqr2d@}\), so IQR \(\approx {@iqrd@}\) litres. [[if test="is(abs(iqralt-iqr)>0.0501)"]]Subtracting the rounded quartiles gives \({@q3d@} - {@q1d@} = {@iqraltd@}\); that small rounding difference is also accepted.[[else]]Subtracting the rounded quartiles gives the same value to 1 decimal place.[[/if]]</p><p>(d) QD \(= (Q_3 - Q_1)/2 \approx {@qdd@}\) litres. Half of a rounded IQR, rounded again to 1 decimal place, is also accepted.</p><p>Note: if you used the class limits instead of the boundaries for both quartiles, both are half a litre too large, so the IQR comes out right. The quartiles themselves are still wrong.</p>]]></text>
  </generalfeedback>
